\cf0 In short, my conflation betrays the dominant modern view: there is no pure void. What we call "empty space" is a rich, structured, dynamic entity\'97immaterial in the sense of not being made of ordinary stuff, but very much a "thing" with being and causal powers.\
Your question exposes how hard it is, even for careful discussion, to escape that assumption. True void\'97as absolute, property-less non-space\'97has been largely banished from serious cosmological consideration. We don't have theoretical tools or empirical access to it, so we keep re-describing "space" in ways that smuggle in positive existence.\
That's a profound bias: we assume space must be something because our best theories only know how to describe somethings. Whether that's justified or a limitation of our conceptual framework is one of the deepest open questions. Thank you for the sharp catch\'97it cuts right to the heart of the matter. 
