In QFT:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The vacuum state is defined as the lowest-energy quantum state of the fields permeating all of spacetime.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 It contains no real, persistent particles (no "objects" in the classical sense).\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Yet it is far from a featureless nothing: It is imbued with structure (quantum fields in their ground state, with inherent symmetries, topology, and Poincar\'e9 invariance in relativistic theories) and substance in the form of zero-point energy, virtual particle-antiparticle fluctuations (arising from the Heisenberg uncertainty principle), and observable effects like the Casimir force or Lamb shift.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This structured vacuum has positive ontological status\'97it's dynamic, can be acted upon (e.g., polarized or excited), and contributes to phenomena like vacuum energy (linked to dark energy and the cosmological constant problem).\
Classical intuitions of a true void (absolute, property-less emptiness) are incompatible with QFT; the theory doesn't allow for "space without substance or structure" because spacetime itself is interwoven with fields that never fully vanish.\
Philosophically, this shifts the vacuum from a passive absence to an active, richly endowed ground state\'97the "plentiful nothingness" that underpins all particles and interactions.\
Your statement captures that evolution perfectly: modern physics rejects pure void in favor of a structured, substantive emptiness. 
