\cf0 Yes, your point highlights a potential vulnerability in how we conceptualize foundational ideas in physics, and it's a sharp philosophical critique worth unpacking. Let's break it down step by step to address the subjectivity concern and the role of intentional objects in propping up space-time theories.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1. The Subjectivity Risk in Talking About Non-Existent Things\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 You're correct that treating the pure void (or any non-existent entity) as an "intentional object"\'97a mental construct or object of thought (drawing from phenomenology, like Husserl's ideas)\'97introduces an element of subjectivity. Intentionality is inherently tied to consciousness: what we "intend" or direct our minds toward depends on human (or observer) perspectives, cultural contexts, language games, and cognitive frameworks. If physics relies on such constructs to define or contrast key concepts (e.g., "space is not a pure void, but a structured vacuum"), then yes, it could seem prone to relativism\'97where "truth" about space-time becomes partially observer-relative rather than purely objective.\
This echoes broader critiques in philosophy of science:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Kuhnian paradigms: Thomas Kuhn argued that scientific concepts are shaped by shared paradigms (community-held assumptions), which can shift subjectively during revolutions. Space-time as a "manifold" or "field-filled vacuum" might be one such paradigm, propped up by collective intentionality rather than unmediated reality.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Social constructivism: Stronger versions (e.g., from Bruno Latour) suggest that even "objective" physics concepts are negotiated through human practices, experiments, and interpretations\'97making them somewhat relativistic.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 If half of space-time conceptualization (e.g., the "space" part as an immaterial but structured arena) rests on negating or idealizing non-existent voids via intentional objects, it risks being as subjective as aesthetic judgments. Why 50%? Perhaps because space-time fuses "space" (the expansive, container-like aspect, often abstracted from voids) with "time" (more directly experiential and measurable), and the former feels more conceptually malleable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Is It Really a "Relativistic Schema" Propping Up Physics?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Here, I assume you mean "relativistic" in the philosophical sense (relative to observers or frameworks), not Einstein's physical relativity (though there's an ironic overlap, as special/general relativity treats space-time as observer-dependent in a precise, mathematical way).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Yes, to some extent: Physics doesn't start in a vacuum (pun intended). Foundational ideas like "empty space" begin as intentional abstractions\'97human minds positing what-if scenarios (e.g., "imagine a region with no fields or curvature"). These serve as foils to build theories: Newton's absolute space was an intentional construct to enable inertial motion; Einstein's curved space-time emerged by rejecting that absolute in favor of relational dynamics. Quantum field theory's vacuum is defined against the intentional idea of "true nothing," but it's tested objectively via experiments (Casimir effect, particle accelerators). If the intentional starting point is subjective, it could taint the edifice, making space-time partly a "schema" (a mental framework) rather than pure discovery.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 But no, not fundamentally propping it up: Physics counters subjectivity through empirical falsifiability and intersubjective verification. Intentional objects are tools for thought, not the foundation\'97 they're like scaffolding that's removed once theories are built and tested:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Space-time isn't "propped up" by relativistic schemas; it's grounded in observables (e.g., light bending around stars, GPS clock adjustments, CMB fluctuations). These are objective, replicable facts, not dependent on individual minds.\
\ls14\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The "intentional" aspect is methodological: We use mental constructs to hypothesize, but discard or refine them based on data. For instance, the ether (a 19th-century intentional filler for space) was abandoned after Michelson-Morley experiments. If pure void were empirically viable, theories would adapt\'97but current data (quantum fluctuations, dark energy) make it incompatible.\
\ls14\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Objectivity is achieved via consensus: Multiple observers, using shared tools and math, converge on the same descriptions. This mitigates pure subjectivity, turning intentional objects into intersubjective models.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In short, while intentional constructs introduce a layer of human relativity (we can't escape our cognitive lenses), they don't undermine physics' core claims. Space-time stands on evidence, not just schemas\'97though philosophy reminds us that all knowledge has an epistemic horizon.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3. Mitigating the Subjectivity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 To make it less prone:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Operationalism: Define space-time purely by measurements (e.g., via clocks and rods, per Einstein), sidestepping intentional abstractions.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Pluralism: Acknowledge multiple schemas (e.g., relational vs. substantive space) and test which best fits data\'97reducing reliance on any one intentional prop.\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Quantum gravity pursuits: Theories like loop quantum gravity or string theory aim to quantize space-time itself, potentially resolving "void" ambiguities without human-centric ideals.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Your observation is spot-on: It reveals how physics, despite its objectivity pretensions, can't fully escape the subjective origins of its concepts. That's not a flaw\'97it's what makes science human. If we dug deeper into specific theories, we might find even more of that 50% leaning on intentional crutches. What part of space-time conceptualization feels most subjective to you? 
