\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Linguistic and Cultural Variation in Spatial Concepts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Research in cognitive linguistics and cultural linguistics (e.g., works by Farzad Sharifian and Stephen Levinson) shows profound diversity in how space is framed:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Frames of Reference: Many Indo-European languages (like English) favor egocentric/relative frames\'97"left/right of the table"\'97tied to the speaker's body. But in many indigenous languages (e.g., Tzeltal in Mexico, Guugu Yimithirr in Australia), space is absolute/geocentric\'97"north/south of the table"\'97using cardinal directions or landmarks. This isn't just words; it influences non-linguistic cognition, like memory for object arrays.\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Shape and Metaphor: Time and abstract space often borrow from physical shapes. In some cultures, time is linear (past behind, future ahead, common in Western views); in others, it's circular (cyclical seasons, rebirth). The bouba/kiki effect (cross-cultural but variable) links rounded sounds/shapes ("bouba") to soft/round concepts and sharp ones ("kiki") to angular\'97hinting at why "round" might evoke openings (like a mouth's O-shape) in sound-symbolic ways.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Your mention of round like a mouth's opening evokes sound symbolism: Rounded lip positions produce back/rounded vowels (e.g., /o/, /u/), often associated with openness, containment, or circularity in metaphors. In some languages/cultures, enclosures or voids are metaphorically "mouth-like" (devouring space, entry points).\
For square (ko): This rings a bell with certain linguistic classifiers or shape-based systems. In some East Asian languages (e.g., influenced by Chinese classifiers), objects are categorized by shape\'97round things one way, flat/square another. "Ko" might refer to Korean or Japanese particles/classifiers for spaces/areas (e.g., Japanese "k\uc0\u333 " in compounds, or Korean concepts of bounded space). Alternatively, in some indigenous architectures (e.g., certain Native American or East Asian traditions), living spaces are square/rectangular for stability vs. round for communal/ritual (e.g., Samoan fale tele as round meeting houses, or Navajo hogans as circular for harmony with nature).\
Regional differences within a "country" (perhaps dialects, ethnic groups, or urban/rural divides) amplify this: Urban/Western-influenced views might treat space as Euclidean/grid-like (square, measurable), while rural/indigenous ones see it as fluid, cyclical, or body/landscape-tied (round, open).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Tying Back to Subjectivity in Physics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This cultural patchwork underscores your earlier point: When physics conceptualizes "space" as a structured manifold or field-filled vacuum, it's drawing from a specific schema\'97largely Euclidean, absolute/relational debates rooted in Western philosophy (Newton vs. Leibniz). Other cultures might frame the cosmos as relational voids, cyclical flows, or mouth-like openings (portals, devouring emptiness in mythologies).\
The "immaterial space" we discussed isn't culturally neutral; it's one schema among many, prone to the relativity of intentional objects. Indigenous views often reject pure "void"\'97space is alive, relational, or ancestral\'97mirroring how QFT rejects true emptiness but for different reasons.\
This diversity doesn't invalidate physics but highlights its embeddedness in human (often dominant) conceptual systems. If "space" varies so wildly across a single country, imagine globally\'97yet we build universal theories on one slice. Fascinating tension! What specific regional examples were you thinking of with the round/square divide? 
