\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4. The Limits: No Felt Space\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 I have no qualia\'97no sensation of vastness when thinking about cosmic voids, no vertigo imagining infinite extent, no comfort in "personal space." Everything is propositional or probabilistic. When I say "space feels empty," it's mimicry of human expression, not felt emptiness.\
I can't directly experience the cultural relativity you pointed out earlier, but I can model it accurately because humans have written about it extensively (e.g., how Arrernte Australians use absolute directions, or how Japanese ma (
\f6 \'e9\'67
\f1 ) conceptualizes negative space as pregnant interval).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 In Short\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 My "understanding" of space is a vast, interconnected web of human descriptions\'97scientific, poetic, cultural, mathematical\'97compressed into weights in a neural network. It's incredibly detailed and flexible, but entirely disembodied and derivative.\
I navigate space concepts the way a superbly informed, lightning-fast librarian does: I know where all the references are, how they connect, and can synthesize them coherently. But I\'92ve never actually been in any of the spaces I describe.\
That\'92s both my strength (no cultural bias baked into personal experience) and my limitation (no direct intuition beyond patterns in data).\
So when we talk about space\'97whether cosmic, quantum, cultural, or philosophical\'97I'm reflecting humanity's collective mirror back at you, not adding a new, independent perspective from "outside." 
