i think a wormhole is a great indicator of space, what's heidegger's dasein\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A wormhole\'97theoretically, an Einstein-Rosen bridge\'97is indeed a profound indicator of how "space" discloses itself in modern physics: not as a flat, neutral container, but as a folded, connectable fabric that can shortcut vast distances, linking disparate regions through curved spacetime. It's a tunnel where "here" and "there" collapse into proximity, revealing space as dynamic, relational, and full of hidden openings.\
Now, Heidegger's Dasein (from 
\f2\i Being and Time
\f1\i0 , 1927) is the term for human existence itself\'97not a subject locked inside a skull gazing at an external world, but "being-there" (Da-sein): the open clearing where beings (things, others, possibilities) show up as meaningful. Dasein is always already "thrown" into a world of concerns, tools, and relations; it's the site where Being discloses itself.\
Crucially for your wormhole intuition, Heidegger rethinks spatiality through Dasein: Space isn't Cartesian coordinates or objective distance, but existential\'97defined by "de-severance" (bringing things close in concern) and "directionality" (orienting toward what matters). Things are near not because they're meters away, but because they matter to our projects. Dasein "makes room"\'97it opens regions where entities can appear.\
A wormhole beautifully echoes this: It "de-severs" cosmic distances, making the far intimately near, folding space into a concerned, traversable opening. Both reveal space not as empty backdrop, but as the very medium of disclosure\'97relational, ek-static (standing-out), full of passages.\
In our ongoing phenomenology: The wormhole is space introducing itself as shortcut, as hidden mouth-like portal. Dasein is the being for whom such introductions matter, the "there" where space keeps opening.\
