\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Muscular Judaism: A Modern "Myth" of Jewish Physicality\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One prominent concept that emerged in the late 19th century is "Muscular Judaism" (Muskeljudentum), coined by Zionist leader Max Nordau at the Second Zionist Congress in 1898. This isn't ancient folklore but a cultural "myth" or ideal aimed at countering stereotypes of Jewish weakness or intellectualism (the "Nebbish" archetype\'97timid, bookish, and physically frail). Nordau promoted physical fitness, gymnastics, and sports to foster a "Muscle Jew" image: broad-shouldered, upright, and strong, embodying Zionist renewal. This influenced movements like Maccabi sports clubs and even modern Israeli culture, but it's more socio-political than mythical. It's not a "folklore myth" per se, but a constructed narrative to reclaim Jewish masculinity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Biblical and Folklore Prohibitions: The Sciatic Nerve and Thigh Muscle\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 In ancient Jewish lore, there's a direct myth tied to muscles via the story of Jacob wrestling with an angel (or divine being) in Genesis 32. After the struggle, Jacob's hip is wrenched, leading to the Jewish prohibition against eating the sciatic nerve (gid hanasheh) or thigh muscle of animals. This is commemorated in kosher laws to this day, symbolizing Jacob's transformation into Israel and the idea of divine struggle leaving a permanent mark on the body. Rabbinic interpretations (e.g., in the Talmud) link it to resilience and spiritual strength, but it's more about injury and restraint than muscle worship. It's a "myth" in the sense of a foundational story explaining a cultural practice.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Giants and Strong Figures in Jewish Mythology\
