\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Jewish folklore is rich with giants (Nephilim or Rephaim), who embody superhuman strength and often relate to themes of power or divine rebellion. The Nephilim are described in Genesis 6 as "giants" or "fallen ones," offspring of divine beings and human women, associated with immense size and might. They were semi-divine, heroic figures wiped out in the Flood, symbolizing hubris or forbidden unions. Later texts like the Book of Enoch expand them into fallen angels or watchers, emphasizing their gigantic stature and strength.\
