He did not arrive with fanfare or prophecy. He simply 
\f2\i was
\f1\i0 . The trees knew him. The animals trusted him. The first mortals who stumbled into the grove felt the same pull \'97 a deep, instinctive safety that made them linger, rest, and eventually build small shrines of stacked stone and woven branches. They called him the Bear-Father, the Warm Root, the one who never turned anyone away.\
For centuries he lived as the grove\'92s silent guardian. He would sit for days on end beneath the oldest pine, whittling small figures from fallen branches \'97 bears, thunderclouds, simple hands holding other hands. The shavings curled golden in the firelight he kept burning for travelers. He offered stew made from roots and game, mead fermented from wild honey, and a place by the fire where no one had to explain their burdens. His touch was never rushed. When a lost soul needed holding, he would wrap them in his arms, the thick fur of his chest a living blanket, the low rumble in his throat a wordless reassurance that they were safe.\
But even the warmest hearth can feel lonely when no one stays.\
Ursalon carried that ache quietly. He watched generations come and go, mortals finding comfort in his grove only to leave when spring returned. He never asked them to remain. He simply gave what he had, and when they departed, he would return to his whittling, the knife moving slow across the wood as if carving out the shape of something he could not yet name.\
The change began the night Grokthar arrived.\
The thunder god stood in the grove like a storm given flesh \'97 dark, electric, swollen with generative power that made the air crackle. Ursalon felt the pull immediately: something vast and untamed that needed rooting, something that could finally match the depth of warmth he had carried alone for so long. Their first touch was gentle, almost hesitant. Then the bond snapped into place \'97 thunder meeting coziness, storm finding fertile ground.\
From that moment, Ursalon\'92s body began to change.\
The transformation was slow, almost reverent. His chest swelled fuller, the fur growing thicker and richer, the muscle beneath yielding softer yet heavier, as if the earth itself was reshaping him to better hold the thunder that had chosen him. His thighs thickened, the inner fur becoming a plush cradle. His cock grew heavier, the generative urge deepening into something primal and insatiable. The caretaker instinct that had always lived in him bloomed into something fiercer \'97 a beastly protectiveness that made him want to isolate and envelop, to surround Grokthar completely so nothing could touch what was his.\
